e to have flexibility  again you can't say whoops I forgot my  pipe patent you better have it with you  or be able to use what you do have and  it better be reliable so these are  completely different challenges than my  colleagues and other synthetic biology  labs have when they need to look towards  Earthlings so what's the big idea well  for millennia we've used biology to do  chemistry on planet earth there is  absolutely no reason we shouldn't  continue to use biology to do chemistry  beyond Earth we may use synthetically  altered organisms for material  production including habitat  construction thought I'll give you some  examples in a moment we may use material  that's brought from the earth to get  started but we may also be repurposing  spacecraft use materials that we find a  destination as the example that I gave  you on Mars was was alluding to or maybe  we pick up materials from an asteroid  and route  we're going to use nanotechnology and  this is kind of an interesting one I get  invited to talk at interesting  conferences including this the opening  speaker for nanotechnology conference a  couple years ago and I must say the  night before in my hotel room was saying  what am I doing here  I couldn't even pass a freshman glass  and nanotechnology and then I suddenly  realize every cell in your body is a  better nanotechnologist that any person  who is in that room because we have to  do nanotechnology at an incredibly  precise level or we're not alive and so  if you use biology to do the  nanotechnology think about making DNA  RNA making proteins this requires  incredible precision or you're no longer  alive so we can use biology do  nanotechnology we may have alternate  biochemistry everyone in this room uses  right-handed sugars and left-handed  amino acids why not make a mirror  organism so we know who our worker  organisms are who's us and who's them  for example we may send coding  information digitally to Mars to make to  make drugs or whatever